# sshape mode=grow face=yes s=yes stages="today;|938;" capAt=.25
Why is Vietnam shaped like an S?
Because a thousand years ago… it was a blob.
In the next few minutes: two royal weddings, a rescue at sea, a kingdom that disappeared…
…and the reason your train from Hà Nội to Sài Gòn takes thirty-four hours.

# title variant=film place=village text="WHY IS VIETNAM|AN S?" sub="Ep. 3" trans=fgwipe
Why is Vietnam an S?

# chapter num=1 text="The blob"
Chapter one. The blob.

# sshape mode=grow face=yes stages="938;Independence"
Year 938. After about a thousand years under Chinese rule, Đại Việt wins its independence.
Its home is the Red River Delta: flat, wet, and perfect for rice.
And rice means people. Lots of people.

# dialogue a=dave b=copho place=sidewalk voiceA=am_puck voiceB=af_bella act="copho:wipe" lines="a:thinking:So Vietnam was just… the top bit?|b:smug:The top bit, yes.|a:shocked:Where's the rest?|b:neutral:Someone else's."

# chapter num=2 text="Why south?"
Chapter two. Why south?

# sshape mode=terrain stages="938;Boxed in" sfx="0.4:rumble:0.4"
Look at the neighbours. North: China. Not an option.
West: the Trường Sơn, a wall of mountains about eleven hundred kilometres long.
East: the sea.
Three quarters of today's Vietnam is hills and mountains. Rice hates hills.
So there's exactly one direction with flat, wet land: south, down a thin strip of coast.

# chapter num=3 text="Champa"
Chapter three. Champa.

# talk place=champa host=cheman mood=neutral pose=present shots="WS cheman|MS cheman" cam=crane fg=flowers
Only problem? That coast already belonged to someone.
The kingdom of *Champa*: sailors and traders for over a thousand years…
…linking the Indian Ocean to East Asia, and building red-brick temples to Shiva.

# sshape mode=grow face=yes stages="938;|1069;A war, three provinces" trans=lightleak
For centuries, the two kingdoms raid each other.
Then, in 1069, Đại Việt captures Champa's king. His ransom: three provinces.

# chapter num=4 text="The wedding"
Chapter four. The wedding.

# moment kind=split a=court b=champa labels="ĐẠI VIỆT|CHAMPA" heroA=anhtong heroB=cheman timeA=day timeB=sunset
1306. Two kings. One deal.

# dialogue a=cheman b=anhtong place=court voiceA=bm_george voiceB=am_fenrir lines="a:neutral:I'd like to marry Princess Huyền Trân.|b:thinking:And the gift?|a:smug:Two provinces.|b:thinking:Which two?|a:neutral:Ô and Lý.|b:joy:…Deal."

# sshape mode=grow face=yes stages="1069;|1306;Ô & Lý → Huế"
The border slides south again.
Those two provinces? Today, that's the land around Huế.
Most expensive wedding gift in history. And then… the king died.

# chapter num=5 text="The rescue"
Chapter five. The rescue.

# talk place=champa time=sunset host=huyentran mood=sad pose=stand shots="WS huyentran|MS huyentran" cam=pushIn
In 1307, barely a year after the wedding, Chế Mân dies.
And Cham royal custom says a king's wives follow him onto the funeral pyre.

# dialogue a=khacchung b=huyentran place=nightsea time=night voiceA=am_michael voiceB=af_nicole lines="a:determined:Princess. We came for the funeral.|b:nervous:You came for the funeral?|a:determined:Officially.|b:surprised:And unofficially?|a:smug:We have a very fast boat."

# sshape mode=grow face=yes stages="1306;The long way home" mood=shock
Đại Việt's envoy, Trần Khắc Chung, smuggles her out by sea. The trip home takes a year.
Champa is furious. And the wars come back.

# chapter num=6 text="The fall of Vijaya"
Chapter six. The fall of Vijaya.

# sshape mode=grow face=yes stages="1306;|1471;Vijaya falls" mood=shock
And Champa hits back hard.
In the 1370s, its king Chế Bồng Nga sacks Đại Việt's capital. More than once.
But in 1471, Emperor Lê Thánh Tông takes Champa's capital, Vijaya.
This part was not a wedding. Tens of thousands are killed or captured.
Champa survives, but only as a small kingdom in the far south.

# chapter num=7 text="The Mekong"
Chapter seven. The Mekong.

# sshape mode=grow face=yes stages="1471;|1611;The Nguyễn lords|1653;Phan Rang|1697;"
By the 1600s, the south is run by the Nguyễn lords, far from the court in the north.
Province by province, settlers keep moving down the coast.
1653: the border reaches Phan Rang. By the 1690s, what's left of Champa is a small vassal state.

# gag place=sidewalk hero=dave mood=happy react=shocked reactPose=scared punch="AGAIN?!" cam=snap capY=.86
And in 1620… another royal marriage. With Cambodia.

# talk place=riverbank host=huucanh mood=determined pose=present shots="WS huucanh|MS huucanh" cam=crane
Princess Ngọc Vạn marries the Cambodian king…
…and the Nguyễn get a foothold at a Khmer fishing village called Prey Nokor.
In 1698, Nguyễn Hữu Cảnh arrives to set up an administration there. Its new name: Gia Định.
You know it as Sài Gòn.

# sshape mode=grow face=yes s=yes stages="1697;|1698;Prey Nokor → Sài Gòn|1757;The Mekong Delta|today;"
1708: a Chinese émigré, Mạc Cửu, pledges the port of Hà Tiên to the Nguyễn.
1757: the last lands of the Mekong Delta.
And the S… is complete.

# chapter num=8 text="The cost"
Chapter eight. The cost.

# talk place=towers host=cham mood=neutral pose=present shots="WS cham|MS cham|CU cham" cam=crane fg=leaves
In Vietnamese history books, this is the *Nam tiến*: the March to the South.
For the Cham and the Khmer, it's the story of losing a homeland. Both are true.
Champa's last territory was annexed in 1832.
But Champa didn't vanish.
About one hundred and eighty thousand Cham live in Vietnam today, and their towers still stand.
And the Mekong Delta is home to more than one point three million Khmer…
…with their own pagodas, language and festivals.

# chapter num=9 text="What it means for your trip"
Chapter nine. What it means for your trip.

# dialogue a=copho b=dave place=sidewalk voiceA=af_bella voiceB=am_puck lines="a:smug:Guess how wide Vietnam is at the waist.|b:thinking:Five hundred kilometres?|a:smug:Lower.|b:surprised:Two hundred?|a:laugh:Fifty."

# sshape mode=narrow label="≈ 50 KM" sub="at the waist, near Đồng Hới"
At its narrowest, near Đồng Hới, Vietnam is about fifty kilometres wide. That's where Phong Nha's caves are.

# sshape mode=length trans=burn label="1,650 KM" sub="north tip → Cà Mau"
Top to bottom? About one thousand six hundred and fifty kilometres.
Plus more than three thousand kilometres of coast.
Long country, three climates.
In winter, Hà Nội sits around fifteen to twenty degrees.
Huế gets its heaviest rain.
Sài Gòn is twenty-eight to thirty-two, and dry.
So pack for all three.

# route stops="hanoi,hue,danang,hoian,nhatrang,hcmc" stats="1,726 km;by rail|33–37 h;Hà Nội → Sài Gòn|≈ 2 h;by plane" hero=dave vehicle=train
By train, Hà Nội to Sài Gòn is one thousand seven hundred kilometres: thirty-three to thirty-seven hours.
Fly, and it's about two hours.

# moment kind=window hero=dave time=sunset expr=love capY=.2
A sleeper berth runs about [1.2 to 1.8 million ₫] (~$45–70). Book a few weeks ahead around Tết.

# list host=cham place=towers title="SEE CHAMPA TODAY" items="Mỹ Sơn · near Hội An (UNESCO)|Po Nagar · Nha Trang|Po Klong Garai · Phan Rang"
Want to see what's left of Champa?
Mỹ Sơn, near Hội An. Po Nagar, in Nha Trang. Po Klong Garai, near Phan Rang.

